From: haiku-rpi
Subject: [PATCH] efi_loader: hand out cleared pages from efi_allocate_pages()

AllocatePages()/AllocatePool() returned memory as it was in the RAM. With a
random fill of the RAM before booting (what a cold start leaves), Haiku booted
over the network froze 3 of 3 warm boots; the culprit page, found by
bisecting, was the pool page of a debug image info table entry (24 bytes
used, the rest of the page as found). Clearing what efi_allocate_pages()
hands out (pools come from it too): alive 3 of 3.
---
diff --git a/lib/efi_loader/efi_memory.c b/lib/efi_loader/efi_memory.c
index c3da7c20..87d87df7 100644
--- a/lib/efi_loader/efi_memory.c
+++ b/lib/efi_loader/efi_memory.c
@@ -500,6 +500,16 @@ efi_status_t efi_allocate_pages(enum efi_allocate_type type,
 		return EFI_OUT_OF_RESOURCES;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Hand out cleared memory. The specification leaves the contents
+	 * undefined, but an EFI application that relies on zeroes - or on the
+	 * slack of a pool allocation - then sees whatever was in the RAM:
+	 * garbage after a cold start, the previous boot's bytes after a warm
+	 * one. Haiku on a Raspberry Pi 4 hung early in its kernel that way
+	 * (a page of the debug image info table's pool). Clearing costs
+	 * little next to loading the image into these pages.
+	 */
+	memset((void *)(uintptr_t)efi_addr, 0, (size_t)pages << EFI_PAGE_SHIFT);
 	*memory = efi_addr;
 
 	return EFI_SUCCESS;
